$V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=6$ and $\\sigma_2=4.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-36I & =[\\begin{aligned}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{aligned}]-36[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 36I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvector\n![](images/20/20ff5eb3c00c496739a4938d51819c523f35dd9631674b312b6603c9c998043a.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 16I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvector\n![](images/ea/ea2f862b6140e7997710f842de24776363ea4bda60ecef64ded2a3b8f0710760.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/52/528da30da7d7781485d6c41d0dec0b5a02cc94bae1b27895908a2b470d262ef5.png)" 13194139534512 17592186045417 false] [17592186161835 1074 "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $