g)" 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186161836 1071 "![](images/56/56923d0bc8f47d504837e3dca2f7191cf3d5d571245ee4453c8d1272dfb1dbd3.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find a $3\\times 3$ matrix that could be the matrix $U.$" 13194139534512 17592186045417 false] [17592186161836 1071 "![](images/b9/b9a8dca1b62edcadcef99761c807fb080c90b361e13d1a1d85a3b76ee11c2327.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find a $3\\times 3$ matrix that could be the matrix $U.$" 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186161836 1074 "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=\\sqrt5$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$ \nAlso, the columns $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, and we do not have a third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n![](images/ad/adcd255d41a153210074a9ce091f40a65faeea884f2462275058ddf057b821f6.png)\n\nsuch that $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0$ and $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|=1.$\n\nSolving $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} x_1 + \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} x_2 = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1 + 2x_2 = 0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n![](images/cb/cb7815fe5b5010a29a93a97bede16e26a7a0382a0d8c8f1c2a3af85762d24f00.png)\n\nwhere $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. Notice that the two linearly independent solutions are mutually orthogonal. Normalizing these vectors, we get \n\n![](images/bb/bbbb111ac90a0d42ab31aa78b0e36d2bdd00436e9f0fbabde2342120f32e1120.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c3/c348424c58cf218e1e4fc0eb3c3efa27517c6a24ac786146f0543f945f31af33.png)" 13194139534512 17592186045417 false] [17592186161836 1074 "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=\\sqrt5$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$ \nAlso, the columns $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, and we do not have a third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence