ed}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n![](images/32/32e17465de4bba33105749d691af2ada0844faad000d5bb2ff8e8ed8d8e045e7.png)\n\nsuch that $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0$ and $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|=1.$\n\nSolving $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2} x_1 + \\dfrac{1}{2} x_2- \\dfrac{1}{2} x_3 = 0   \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\sqrt2x_1 + x_2-x_3 = 0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n![](images/eb/ebd9c8f2b5e96799c652c39f5859e702f9d069e2842099429d4640010c8882fa.png)\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n![](images/52/523cb0ebfa47497a9753cf1dd447db414aa20feb68380878cbe93d04ec407ad7.png)\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing the orthogonal vectors $\\mathbf{c}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U{:}$   \n\n![](images/6b/6bbe1a671b976508c3a5b25e11f0bec804ae0425afdec8ea5046130145eb69c0.png)\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n![](images/17/17552120ad9a7ef3e6a6b1170a88fc104758254f9f7d1ade70a7f7a833deac94.png)" 13194139534512 17592186045417 false] [17592186161839 1074 "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=2$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$\nAlso, the columns $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n![](images/a1/a1256c5e8c454d905c1550c06bf3aa413cc17acaeba0e6e2cbbda68cfc05ac50.svg)\n\nsuch that $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0$ and $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|=1.$\n\nSolving $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2} x_1 + \\dfrac{1}{2} x_2- \\dfrac{1}{2} x_3 = 0   \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\sqrt2x_1 + x_2-x_3 = 0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n![](images/a6/a619cda4912587e8c4dc4da0bc7d0a575d1c10f1adba2137099f347f6d7eabb3.svg)\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n![](images/22/22311430e9529d48b9d0460011e6baceacfbfcc54902439e917bfdc3dd04d5db.svg)\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing the orthogonal vectors $\\mathbf{c}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U{:}$   \n\n![](images/66/661e2d509c842bac2a5427e82c3973feb972b540dbf405be7a023a7802cf93fb.svg)\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n![](images/1a/1a27507e18b6bcb0353668919f7fb5c356afa1f07ab77f73422fe3236e78fe8a.svg)" 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186162041 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423860 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186162041 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423862 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186162041 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423865 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186162042 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423863 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186162043 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423859 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186162043 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423864 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186162045 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423858 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186162045 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423861 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186162227 1050 1500.0 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186162713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186162713 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrix $V?$" 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186162713 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the matrix $V$?" 13194139534512 17592186045417 false] [17592186162713 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-10I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-0I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V:$\n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186163233 1064 "We can compute a singular value decomposition (SVD) of any matrix! The algorithm for computing an SVD of a rectangular $m\\times n$ matrix $(m\\neq n)$ is similar to the algorithm for square matrices.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/f6/f65e0eee905da85ce0be4bbf39546c2b31c2bac082ff00a6a1d54fd5b89a4a4e.svg)\n\nNotice that $A$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix. So, to compute the SVD of $A,$ we must write\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{blue}{3}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.**  Find the singular values of $A.$ Computing the eigenvalues of $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 4, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1} =  2,  \\qquad \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} =  \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T A$). Seeking non-zero solutions of $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_1 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ and $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_2 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvectors \n![](images/07/07085f021218ff17060b3c1a9099e1cabad24b3d12269f97f1fe50d49b970f6b.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A.$ Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the first two columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $U$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix. However, we do not have the third singular value, so we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n![](images/71/71f89dbcee33bce284b3085b872fdb9da2cc41166fe8a0bd26ef0bb5fe6876aa.svg) \nsuch that \n![](images/9e/9edc744372650e86c27f31ef15ac575cc8203803bdc5fe5730390727fd37146a.svg)\nSolving this system, we get the following nonzero normalized solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c2/c2bbd771d886da6b3a369b7e03345d1be75c18c26c66590598581d2d99590524.svg)" 13194139534512 17592186045417 true] [17592186163233 1064 "We can compute a singular value decomposition (SVD) of any matrix! The algorithm for computing an SVD of a rectangular $m\\times n$ matrix $(m\\neq n)$ is similar to the algorithm for square matrices.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix. So, to compute the SVD of $A,$ we must write\n\n$$A=U \\Sigma V^T,$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n- $U$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{blue}{3}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}$ matrix,\n- $V$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values of $A.$ Computing the eigenvalues of $A^TA,$ we get $$\\lambda_1 = 4, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = 2.$$ Therefore, the singular values of $A$ are $$\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1} = 2, \\qquad \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} = \\sqrt{2}.$$\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T A$). Seeking non-zero solutions of $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_1 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ and $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_2 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvectors $$\\ma